      "text": "Hello sir, as an independent researcher of memecoin history, I've been following your project, Cope. After seventeen months of your hard work promoting it, it's finally getting a response. I'm not curious about why it suddenly blew up, nor am I curious about which top figures are backing it. I'm just curious about one thing: what kept you building and consistently tweeting when the market cap was practically zero? I discussed this with a friend and offered my own theory. From a normal person's perspective, I can understand supporting a coin, and I can also understand doing it purely for the meme culture. But shouldn't that be based on at least some level of market cap or reach? Or perhaps the tweeting wasn't as frequent? I've observed many tokens with near-zero market caps, and even if some people are still holding on, they rarely last that long, and they certainly aren't that active. So, in my view, this doesn't follow the typical logic of launching a coin. However, I don't deny that there are some extremely passionate meme culture enthusiasts who are willing to pour their energy and time into their creations with no expectation of return. Perhaps you are one of them? I have another theory: are you conducting an experiment? This is a bold idea, and perhaps a bit absurd. I've seen a lot of performance art, like Tehching Hsieh taking a self-portrait every hour, 24/7 for a year; or Francis Alÿs pushing a block of ice for a day until it completely melted. These acts seem absurd and illogical. So when countless people found your promotion baffling, I thought of an artistic experiment. Perhaps you were testing whether continuously shilling a coin over a long period would eventually attract attention? Or maybe you were researching some ideas of your own. In any case, I find what you're doing very interesting, and today's success must have given you some answers. Of course, if you were just doing it for the hype, you are successful！",
